# ====== Code Summary ======
# create the `audit_log` table — the append-only trail of every mutating /api/v1 action
#
# Revision: b7d3f1a8c204
# Revises: a3f7c2e91b64
# Created: 2026-09-02 00:00:00.000000
#
# Adds one new table, ``audit_log``: one immutable row per mutating API action (who/when/what/outcome),
# written by a request-scoped middleware and read back by a root-only, paginated+filtered endpoint.
#
# Key design choices:
# - PK is a BIGINT IDENTITY (GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY), NOT the schema's usual app-generated
#   UUID v4. This table is append-heavy and is never the target of a foreign key, so a monotonic
#   server-generated key gives sequential btree insert locality on the hot INSERT path (no random-UUID
#   page-split churn) and a stable tiebreaker for keyset pagination when many rows share one
#   ``created_at`` under a burst (ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC).
# - The actor columns (``actor_user_id``, ``actor_key_id``) carry NO foreign key. An audit row is a
#   permanent historical fact: it must survive — and must never block — deletion of the user/key it
#   references, and it keeps the raw actor id for forensics after that actor is gone. ``actor_label``
#   is the human-readable fallback so the log reads without joins.
# - ``path`` stores the low-cardinality ROUTE TEMPLATE ("/api/v1/collections/{id}/reingest"), not the
#   concrete path, so ``(target_type, target_id, ...)`` — parsed by the middleware — carries the id.
#
# Five read-path btree indexes back the endpoint's filters + the age-based retention prune: a global
# ``created_at DESC``; per-actor ``(actor_key_id|actor_user_id, created_at DESC)``; per-resource
# ``(target_type, target_id, created_at DESC)``; and a plain ``correlation_id``. The DESC composites
# use the ``text("created_at DESC")`` form already proven to reconcile cleanly under ``--autogenerate``
# on ``job``/``document``, so they are also declared on the model's ``__table_args__``.
#
# Data safety: STRICTLY ADDITIVE and fully reversible. The upgrade only CREATEs a brand-new table and
# its indexes; no existing table, column, constraint, or data is touched. The downgrade DROPs exactly
# that table (and, with it, its indexes) — irreversible only in the ordinary sense that it discards the
# audit rows accumulated since the upgrade, which is expected for dropping a log table.

